---
import PlaygroundShell from '../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Simulate it" hint="Four versions of the same small codebase: an order service, an invoice service and a reminder service that all send mail and read orders. Pick a design and a change, and see what the change touches. The numbers come from the dependency data shown below the boxes.">
  <ol class="how">
    <li>Start with the defaults, <b>Hard-wired</b> and <b>Swap the mail provider</b>: every service names the old mailer, so the swap touches <b>4</b> files, <b>3</b> of them high-level. <b>10</b> tests must be re-run and <b>11</b> tests need real infrastructure.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Design</b> to <b>DI of concrete types</b>: the mailer is now passed in, yet the swap touches <b>5</b> files, one more than before, because the constructors and the entry point name the concrete class. Then set <b>Design</b> to <b>Owned abstraction</b>: the swap touches <b>2</b> files, no high-level file, and only <b>2</b> tests need real infrastructure.</li>
    <li>Set <b>Design</b> to <b>Interface for everything</b> and <b>Change</b> to <b>Change the high-level rule</b>: the codebase has <b>16</b> files, and a one-rule change now touches <b>3</b> files and re-runs <b>6</b> tests, against <b>1</b> file with an owned abstraction. Set <b>Change</b> to <b>Add one test of OrderService</b>: a reader follows <b>5</b> layers to understand it, against <b>3</b>.</li>
  </ol>
  <div class="pg dip" data-dip>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <label>Design <select class="pg-in" data-design><option value="0" selected>Hard-wired</option><option value="1">DI of concrete types</option><option value="2">Owned abstraction</option><option value="3">Interface for everything</option></select></label>
      <label>Change <select class="pg-in" data-change><option value="swap" selected>Swap the mail provider</option><option value="test">Add one test of OrderService</option><option value="rule">Change the high-level rule</option></select></label>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-grid">
      <div class="pg-box" data-b1><h3>Files touched</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o1></span><span class="pg-note" data-n1></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b2><h3>High-level files touched</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o2></span><span class="pg-note" data-n2></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b3><h3>Tests to re-run</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o3></span><span class="pg-note" data-n3></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b4><h3>Tests needing real infrastructure</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o4></span><span class="pg-note" data-n4></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b5><h3>Layers to understand</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o5></span><span class="pg-note" data-n5></span></div>
      <div class="pg-box" data-b6><h3>Touch points</h3><span class="pg-mono" data-o6></span><span class="pg-note" data-n6></span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--border)">
      <h3>The same change in all four designs</h3>
      <div class="dip-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="The same change in all four designs, as a table"><table class="dip-table" data-table></table></div>
      <span class="pg-note" data-verdict></span>
    </div>
    <div class="pg-box" style="--c: var(--border)">
      <h3>Source dependencies in this design</h3>
      <span class="pg-note" data-deps-note></span>
      <ol class="pg-log" data-deps tabindex="0" aria-label="Which file depends on which"></ol>
    </div>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query } from '../playground';
  interface F { name: string; kind: 'root' | 'policy' | 'infra' | 'interface' | 'helper'; uses: string[]; tests: number; impl: string; rule: boolean }
  interface Result { touched: string[]; policy: number; rerun: number; infra: number; layers: number }

  // The same model as the code samples: files with the files they use, the interface a detail implements, and their tests.
  const F = (name: string, kind: F['kind'], uses: string[], tests: number, impl = '', rule = false): F => ({ name, kind, uses, tests, impl, rule });
  const SERVICES = ['OrderService', 'InvoiceService', 'ReminderService'];
  const hard = [F('Program', 'root', SERVICES, 0), F('OrderService', 'policy', ['SmtpMailer', 'SqlDatabase'], 4, '', true),
    F('InvoiceService', 'policy', ['SmtpMailer', 'SqlDatabase'], 3), F('ReminderService', 'policy', ['SmtpMailer'], 2),
    F('SmtpMailer', 'infra', [], 1), F('SqlDatabase', 'infra', [], 1)];
  const concrete = [F('Program', 'root', [...SERVICES, 'SmtpMailer', 'SqlDatabase'], 0), ...hard.slice(1)];
  const owned = [concrete[0], F('OrderService', 'policy', ['Notifier', 'OrderStore'], 4, '', true), F('InvoiceService', 'policy', ['Notifier', 'OrderStore'], 3),
    F('ReminderService', 'policy', ['Notifier'], 2), F('Notifier', 'interface', [], 0), F('OrderStore', 'interface', [], 0),
    F('SmtpMailer', 'infra', [], 1, 'Notifier'), F('SqlDatabase', 'infra', [], 1, 'OrderStore')];
  const everything = [F('Program', 'root', [...SERVICES, 'SmtpMailer', 'SqlDatabase', 'SystemClock', 'OrderValidator', 'PriceCalculator', 'Money'], 0),
    F('OrderService', 'policy', ['Notifier', 'OrderStore', 'IValidator', 'IPriceCalculator', 'IClock'], 4),
    F('InvoiceService', 'policy', ['Notifier', 'OrderStore', 'IMoney', 'IClock'], 3), F('ReminderService', 'policy', ['Notifier', 'IClock'], 2),
    ...owned.slice(4), F('IClock', 'interface', [], 0), F('SystemClock', 'helper', [], 1, 'IClock'), F('IValidator', 'interface', [], 0),
    F('OrderValidator', 'helper', [], 1, 'IValidator'), F('IPriceCalculator', 'interface', [], 0), F('PriceCalculator', 'helper', ['IMoney'], 2, 'IPriceCalculator', true),
    F('IMoney', 'interface', [], 0), F('Money', 'helper', [], 1, 'IMoney')];
  const DESIGNS: [string, F[], string][] = [
    ['Hard-wired', hard, 'Every service creates its own SmtpMailer and SqlDatabase, so each arrow points from a rule down to a detail.'],
    ['DI of concrete types', concrete, 'The details are passed in by the entry point, but the services still name the concrete classes: injection without inversion.'],
    ['Owned abstraction', owned, 'The services depend on Notifier and OrderStore, which they own. SmtpMailer and SqlDatabase point up at those interfaces: the arrows are inverted.'],
    ['Interface for everything', everything, 'As the owned design, plus an interface in front of the clock, the validator, the price rule and even the Money value class, each with a single implementation.'],
  ];

  const get = (d: F[], n: string) => d.find((f) => f.name === n);
  const usersOf = (d: F[], n: string) => d.filter((f) => f.uses.includes(n)).map((f) => f.name);
  function usersClosure(d: F[], names: string[]): string[] {
    const out = [...new Set(names)], todo = [...names];
    while (todo.length) for (const u of usersOf(d, todo.pop()!)) if (!out.includes(u)) { out.push(u); todo.push(u); }
    return out;
  }
  // A test needs real infrastructure when its subject reaches a concrete infra class without passing an interface.
  function reachesInfra(d: F[], n: string): boolean { const f = get(d, n)!; return f.kind === 'infra' || (f.kind !== 'interface' && f.uses.some((u) => reachesInfra(d, u))); }
  function layers(d: F[], n: string, targets: string[] | null): number {
    const f = get(d, n)!, next = [...f.uses, ...(f.kind === 'interface' ? d.filter((x) => x.impl === n).map((x) => x.name) : [])];
    const here = targets === null ? (next.length ? 0 : 1) : (targets.includes(n) ? 1 : 0);
    const deeper = next.map((m) => layers(d, m, targets)).filter((x) => x > 0).map((x) => x + 1);
    return Math.max(here, ...deeper, 0);
  }
  function change(d: F[], kind: string): Result {
    let after = d, touched: string[];
    if (kind === 'swap') {
      touched = ['SendGridMailer', ...usersOf(d, 'SmtpMailer')];
      after = d.filter((f) => f.name !== 'SmtpMailer').map((f) => ({ ...f, uses: f.uses.map((u) => (u === 'SmtpMailer' ? 'SendGridMailer' : u)) }));
      after.push(F('SendGridMailer', 'infra', [], 1, get(d, 'SmtpMailer')!.impl));
    } else if (kind === 'test') { touched = ['NewOrderServiceTest']; after = d.map((f) => (f.name === 'OrderService' ? { ...f, tests: f.tests + 1 } : f)); }
    else {
      const home = d.find((f) => f.rule)!, iface = home.impl ? get(d, home.impl) : undefined;
      touched = [home.name, ...(iface ? [iface.name, ...usersOf(d, iface.name)] : [])];
    }
    const rerun = kind === 'test' ? 1 : usersClosure(d, touched.filter((n) => get(d, n))).reduce((s, n) => s + get(d, n)!.tests, 0) + (kind === 'swap' ? 1 : 0);
    const focus = kind === 'swap' ? ['SendGridMailer'] : kind === 'rule' ? [d.find((f) => f.rule)!.name] : null;
    return { touched, policy: touched.filter((n) => get(after, n)?.kind === 'policy').length, rerun,
      infra: after.filter((f) => reachesInfra(after, f.name)).reduce((s, f) => s + f.tests, 0), layers: layers(after, 'OrderService', focus) };
  }

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-dip]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const setBox = (n: number, value: string, note: string, tone: 'good' | 'bad' | 'mid') => {
      q(`[data-o${n}]`).textContent = value; q(`[data-n${n}]`).textContent = note;
      q<HTMLElement>(`[data-b${n}]`).style.setProperty('--c', tone === 'good' ? 'var(--good)' : tone === 'bad' ? 'var(--bad)' : 'var(--border)');
    };
    // Compared with the best design for this change: best is green, at least double the best is red.
    const tone = (v: number, all: number[]): 'good' | 'bad' | 'mid' => { const m = Math.min(...all); return v === m ? 'good' : v >= 2 * m ? 'bad' : 'mid'; };
    function draw() {
      const di = Number(q<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-design]').value), kind = q<HTMLSelectElement>('[data-change]').value;
      const results = DESIGNS.map(([, d]) => change(d, kind));
      const r = results[di], d = DESIGNS[di][1];
      const col = (f: (x: Result) => number) => results.map(f);
      const points = (x: Result) => x.touched.length + x.rerun;
      setBox(1, `${r.touched.length}`, r.touched.join(', '), tone(r.touched.length, col((x) => x.touched.length)));
      setBox(2, `${r.policy}`, r.policy ? 'rule code is edited for a technical reason, so each edit can break a rule that worked' : 'no business rule is edited for this change', tone(r.policy, col((x) => x.policy)));
      setBox(3, `${r.rerun}`, 'tests of the files touched and of the files that depend on them', tone(r.rerun, col((x) => x.rerun)));
      setBox(4, `${r.infra} of ${d.reduce((s, f) => s + f.tests, 0) + (kind === 'test' ? 1 : 0)}`, 'tests whose subject reaches a real mail server or database with no interface in between', tone(r.infra, col((x) => x.infra)));
      setBox(5, `${r.layers}`, 'files to open on the longest call path that passes through what changes', 'mid');
      setBox(6, `${points(r)}`, 'files touched plus tests re-run', tone(points(r), col(points)));
      const table = q('[data-table]'); table.textContent = '';
      const head = table.insertRow(); ['Design', 'Files in codebase', 'Touched', 'High-level touched', 'Tests re-run', 'Real-infra tests', 'Layers'].forEach((h) => { const th = document.createElement('th'); th.textContent = h; head.appendChild(th); });
      DESIGNS.forEach(([name, df], i) => {
        const x = results[i], row = table.insertRow(); if (i === di) row.className = 'on';
        [name, df.length, x.touched.length, x.policy, x.rerun, x.infra, x.layers].forEach((v) => { row.insertCell().textContent = String(v); });
      });
      q('[data-verdict]').textContent = di === 3 && kind === 'rule'
        ? 'The over-applied design pays here: a rule that is one file elsewhere is spread over an interface, an implementation and a caller.'
        : di === 3 ? 'Interfaces everywhere give the same protection as the owned design for the mail provider, with more files and a longer path to follow.'
        : di === 1 && kind === 'swap' ? 'Passing the class in changed nothing the swap cares about: the constructors name it, and so does the entry point.'
        : di === 2 ? 'Interfaces are drawn only where a detail may change or must be faked. Rule changes stay as small as in the hard-wired code.'
        : 'Compare the other designs in the table: the red cells are the cost of leaving the detail inside the rule.';
      q('[data-deps-note]').textContent = DESIGNS[di][2];
      const list = q('[data-deps]'); list.textContent = '';
      for (const f of d) {
        const arrows = [...f.uses.map((u) => `${f.name} -> ${u}`), ...(f.impl ? [`${f.name} -> ${f.impl} (implements)`] : [])];
        for (const a of arrows) { const li = document.createElement('li'); li.textContent = a; list.appendChild(li); }
      }
    }
    root.addEventListener('change', draw);
    draw();
  });
</script>
<style is:global>
  .playground .dip-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  .playground .dip-table { border-collapse: collapse; font: .82rem var(--font-mono); min-width: 100%; }
  .playground .dip-table th, .playground .dip-table td { padding: 4px 8px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; }
  .playground .dip-table th:first-child, .playground .dip-table td:first-child { text-align: left; }
  .playground .dip-table tr.on td { font-weight: 700; background: var(--accent-soft); }
</style>
